 The episode ends on a lovely, low-key note, with the brothers heading out for beers. After all the running around, they finally get to spend time together without a kidnapping or military cover-up demanding their attention. It's a small moment that Tracker could use more of.
 Colter's family history has always been one of Tracker's most compelling ongoing threads, and keeping Ackles's character around would give it a natural way to explore those secrets. Colter's lone-wolf routine probably isn't going anywhere. But after seeing what happens when Colter has his brother by his side, it would be fun if the series continued this dynamic for a little while longer.
